  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "PS4_010_DEBUG Recursive Lucas Numbers",
   "display_name": "Recursive Lucas Numbers",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>This is a DEBUG-TYPE question. Please read this carefully, it works differently from the other questions.</b></p>\n<p>The answer box below already contains a complete function. For the one example test shown below, this function gives the correct output.</p>\n<p><b>But the function has a logical mistake</b> (possibly more than one). For some other valid inputs, which we have <b>not</b> shown you, it gives a wrong answer or stops with an error.</p>\n<p><b>What you need to do:</b></p>\n<ol><li>Read the code carefully and trace it in your head for different valid inputs, to find the inputs where it goes wrong.</li><li>Fix the mistake(s), so that the function works correctly for <b>all</b> valid inputs described below.</li><li>Add a short comment in the code saying which line(s) you changed and why.</li></ol>\n<p><b>How this question is checked:</b> this question has some <b>hidden tests</b> that check exactly those inputs. When you click <b>Check</b>, we run your code on the example test and on the hidden tests. The pre-filled code will fail at least one hidden test; once you have fixed the mistake(s) correctly, all tests will pass.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><b>The function:</b> <code>lucas(n)</code> takes a non-negative integer (int &gt;= 0) <code>n</code> and <b>returns</b> the n-th <b>Lucas number</b>, using recursion. The Lucas numbers start with 2 and 1, and each number after that is the sum of the two before it:</p>\n<p><code>2, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11, 18, 29, ...</code></p>\n<p>Counting starts from 0, so <code>lucas(0)</code> is 2, <code>lucas(1)</code> is 1, <code>lucas(2)</code> is 3 and <code>lucas(6)</code> is 18.</p>\n<p><b>Your fixed function must work for:</b> any integer <code>n</code> from 0 to 20 (both included).</p>\n<p><b>Example test (already passes):</b> <code>lucas(0)</code> returns <code>2</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# The code below has a logical bug. It will not work correctly for some\n# valid inputs other than the one shown in the example.\n# Identify the BUG and CORRECT it. Add a comment saying what you changed.\n\ndef lucas(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 2\n    else:\n        return lucas(n - 1) + lucas(n - 2)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(lucas(0))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "2",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(lucas(1))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "1",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(lucas(2))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "3",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(lucas(6))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "18",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  }
